<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matilda of Ringelheim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 892–<a href="/years/968">968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Saint Matilda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matilda of Ringelheim was the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>, and mother of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a>. Educated at the abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herford">Herford</a>, she became known for her piety and charity, founded convents including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a> and Nordhausen, and was venerated as a saint after her death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matilda-of-ringelheim.jpg" alt="Matilda of Ringelheim" width="313" height="287" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ptacnik_Mechtilda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Matilda was born around 892, a daughter of Dietrich and Reinhild. Her father was a count in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, and he was descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind">Widukind</a>, who had fought against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. She was raised in Herford Abbey by her grandmother, who was also called Matilda. In 909, when she was seventeen, Matilda married Henry, then duke of Saxony and later king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>. His first marriage, to Hatheburg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a>, had been annulled. About twenty years later, in 929, Henry gave her a dower in the so-called Hausordnung, including land at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a> and Nordhausen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Matilda bore Henry five children. Otto was crowned emperor in 962, Henry became duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 948, and Bruno was elected archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> in 953 and made duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> in 954. As queen, Matilda took an interest in women's monasteries, and her influence on the king's reign is said to have lain in her strong sense of justice.</p><p>Henry died in 936 at Memleben and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a>, where Matilda founded a convent in the same year. She lived there for the following years and looked after the family's legacy. Quedlinburg became a centre of prayer for the kingdom and a place to commemorate its dead, and, like other convents, it educated the daughters of noble families. In 966, after thirty years of leadership, she passed the convent to her granddaughter Matilda, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> and Adelheid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, who became its first abbess. Matilda also founded a convent at Enger in 947 and, in 961, her last foundation, at Nordhausen.</p><p>Between 936 and 946 her son Otto disputed her handling of her dower and claimed her possessions, and she fled into exile. Otto's wife, Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eadgyth">Eadgyth</a>, is said to have brought about a reconciliation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shortly before her death Matilda acquired papal privileges for all the monasteries of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, to protect her legacy. These were overridden after her death, when her dower passed to Theophanu, wife of her grandson Otto II. She died in the convent at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a> on 14 March 968 after a long illness and was buried beside her husband. Two hagiographies and The Deeds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> are the main sources for her life. A plaque to her stands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walhalla">Walhalla memorial</a> near Regensburg, and her feast day is 14 March.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Matilda of Ringelheim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nordhausen grew around a royal palace and a convent founded by <b>Queen Matilda</b> in the 10th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-nordhausen">Free Imperial City of Nordhausen</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Queen Matilda</b>, wife of Henry the Fowler, was educated there.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-herford">Imperial Abbey of Herford</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Enger, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.13,8.57&amp;year=941" aria-label="Enger on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4743 363 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Matilda of Ringelheim"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5193 633h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5193 633h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.13,8.57&amp;year=941">What was here in 941 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
